/* book Worked example: Q: Gas in a cylinder is warmed by an electric heater of 0.50 kW inside the cylinder, switched on for 2.0 minutes. Meanwhile the gas pushes the piston out, doing 25 kJ of work, and 15 kJ of heat leaks through the cylinder wall to the room. Using Q > 0 into the system and W > 0 out of it, find Q, W and Q − W (a) for a boundary that includes the heater, and (b) for a boundary around the gas only. (c) Write the result of (a) in the convention where work on the system is positive.
   1. Energy from the heater: 0.50 kW × (2.0 × 60) s = 0.50 kJ/s × 120 s = 60 kJ.
   2. (a) The heater is inside the boundary, so its 60 kJ enters through the wire as electrical work done on the system, −60 kJ. The piston work is done by the system, +25 kJ. Total W = 25 − 60 = −35 kJ.
   3. The only heat crossing this boundary is the leak to the room: Q = −15 kJ.
   4. Q − W = −15 − (−35) = +20 kJ: a net 20 kJ enters the system.
   5. (b) The heater is outside the boundary. It is hotter than the gas, so its 60 kJ crosses as heat into the gas: Q = +60 − 15 = +45 kJ. The only work is at the piston: W = +25 kJ.
   6. Q − W = 45 − 25 = +20 kJ, the same net energy as in (a).
   7. (c) With work on the system counted positive, the heater work is +60 kJ and the piston work is −25 kJ, so W′ = +35 kJ, while Q = −15 kJ as before. Net energy in = Q + W′ = −15 + 35 = +20 kJ.
   Answer: (a) Q = −15 kJ, W = −35 kJ, Q − W = +20 kJ. (b) Q = +45 kJ, W = +25 kJ, Q − W = +20 kJ. (c) Q = −15 kJ, W′ = +35 kJ, Q + W′ = +20 kJ. The labels depend on the boundary and the convention; the net energy does not. */
